  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "\\(P(D)=1/5\\), \\(P(\\text{positive}\\mid D)=4/5\\), and \\(P(\\text{positive}\\mid\\text{not }D)=1/5\\), and the probability of not having \\(D\\) is \\(4/5\\). Given a positive result, what is the probability of actually having \\(D\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "1/2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Positive results split into two disjoint cases: having \\(D\\) and testing positive has probability \\((1/5)(4/5)=4/25\\), while not having \\(D\\) and testing positive has probability \\((4/5)(1/5)=4/25\\). Together they have probability \\(8/25>0\\). Since the contributions are equal, the conditional probability of having \\(D\\) given a positive result is\n\\[\n\\frac{4/25}{8/25}=\\frac12.\n\\]",
